          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            On this page, we work work through several of the key examples of
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            in the category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Understanding+Constructions+in+Categories"} Understanding Constructions in Categories
          %p
            Recall that a limit, or universal cone, over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:J\to Set
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            such that, given any cone
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone+function"} cone function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T'
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %h2#terminal_object Terminal Object
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            is a universal cone over the empty diagram. In this section, we demonstrate how this leads us to the statement:
          .standout
            %p
              Any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
              (a one-element set) is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            To demonstrate, first note that a cone over an empty diagram is just a set and a corresponding cone function is just a function. Therefore, we are looking for a “universal set”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            such that for an other set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo •
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:C\to\bullet.
          %p
            Singletons fill the bill because for any element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
            we have the unique function defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(c) = *.
          %p
            Therefore any singleton is a terminal object in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %h2#product Product
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            is a universal cone over a discrete diagram. In this section, we demonstrate how this leads us to the statement:
          .standout
            %p
              Any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            To demonstrate, first note that a discrete diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:J\to Set
            produces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family+of+sets"} family of sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A_i)
            with no functions between them. A cone over the discrete diagram consists of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and a single component
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} f
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \f_i:T\to A_i
          %p
            for each set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_i A_i
            such that for any other cone
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:T\to\prod_i A_i.
          %p
            Cartesian product fills the bill because for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(t) = \prod_i f_i(t).
          %p This function is unique because with any other function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:T\to\prod_i A_i
          %p
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i\circ g = f_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(t) = \prod_i f_i(t) = \left(\prod_i f_i\right)(t) = f(t).
          %p
            Therefore
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = g
          %h2#equalizer Equalizer
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            is the universal cone over a parallel diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mo •
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet\rightrightarrows\bullet.
          %p In this section, we demonstrate how this leads us to the statement:
          .standout
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
              of two functions is the subset on which both functions coincide.
          %p
            To demonstrate, first note that a parallel diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:J\to Set
            produces two sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y
            with two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+morphism"} parallel functions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:X\to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and two components
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_X:T\to X\quad\text{and}\quad T_Y:T\to Y.
          %p This is the first example we encounter where the diagram contains morphisms so recall that with a cone we also want the the component diagrams to commute, i.e. we want
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ T_X = T_Y\quad\text{and}\quad g\circ T_X = T_Y.
          %p
            Therefore, we are looking for a universal cone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eq
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq
            such that for any other cone
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Eq
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:T\to Eq.
          %p Let’s first define the set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eq
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq = \left\{x\in X|f(x)=g(x)\right\}
          %p
            with two functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi Eq
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq_X:Eq\to X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mi Eq
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq_Y:Eq\to Y
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq_X(x) = x\quad\text{and}\quad Eq_Y(x) = f(x)
          %p and verify that it is indeed a cone. The only thing we need to check is that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\circ Eq_X = Eq_Y
          %p
            which amounts to showing that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = g(x)
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Eq
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in Eq
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Eq
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq
          %p
            Now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Eq
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi,\psi: T\to Eq
            be two cone functions. For any element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_X(t) = Eq_X\circ\phi(t) = Eq_X\circ\psi(t)\implies \phi(t)=\psi(t)
          %p
            so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = \psi
            and the cone function is unique.
          %p
            Since every cone function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Eq
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:T\to Eq
            is unique, it follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eq
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq
            together with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi Eq
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq_X:Eq\to X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Eq
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mi Eq
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq_Y:Eq\to Y
            is a universal cone, i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Eq
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Eq
            is an equalizer.
          %h2#pullbacks Pullbacks
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            is a universal cone over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \bullet &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \bullet
                  \\\
                  \&amp;
                  \&amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \&amp;
                  \&amp; \swarrow
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\\
                  
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;
                  \\bullet
                  \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;
                  }.
          %p In this section, we demonstrate how this leads us to the statement:
          .standout
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              of two functions of sets is the set of pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)=g(y)
          %p
            To demonstrate, first note that a cospan
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:J\to Set
            produces three sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y,Z
            with two functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Z
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:Y\to Z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and three components
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Z
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_X:T\to X,\quad T_Y:T\to Y,\quad\text{and}\quad T_Z:T\to Z
          %p satisfying
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Z
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Z
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ T_X = T_Z\quad\text{and}\quad g\circ T_Y = T_Z,
          %p which implies, of course,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ T_X = g\circ T_Y.
          %p
            Therefore, we are looking for a universal cone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pb
            such that for any other cone
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Pb
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:T\to Pb.
          %p Let’s first define the set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pb
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pb = \left\{(x,y)|f(x)=g(y)\right\}
          %p
            with three functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi Pb
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_X:Pb\to X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi Pb
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_Y:Pb\to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi Pb
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_X:Pb\to Z
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_X(x,y) = x,\quad\text{and}\quad \pi_Y(x,y) = y,\quad\text{and}\quad T_Z(x,y) = f(x)
          %p and verify that it is indeed a cone. The only thing we need to check is that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ\pi_X = g\circ \pi_Y = T_Z
          %p
            which amounts to showing that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = g(y)
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Pb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)\in Pb
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Pb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pb
          %p
            Now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Eq
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi,\psi: T\to Eq
            be two cone functions with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(t) = (\phi_X(t),\phi_Y(t)\quad\text{and}\quad\psi(t) = (\psi_X(t),\psi_Y(t)
          %p for functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi Y
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_X,\psi_X:T\to X\quad\text{and}\quad\phi_Y,\psi_Y:T\to Y.
          %p
            For any element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_X(t) = \pi_X\circ\phi(t) = \pi_X\circ\psi(t)\implies \phi_X(t)=\psi_X(t).
          %p Similarly
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi Y
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi Y
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_Y(t) = \pi_Y\circ\phi(t) = \pi_Y\circ\psi(t)\implies \phi_Y(t)=\psi_Y(t)
          %p
            so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = \psi
            and the cone function is unique.
          %p
            Since every cone function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Pb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:T\to Pb
            is unique, it follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pb
            together with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi Pb
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_X:Pb\to X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi Pb
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_Y:Pb\to Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Z
                  %mo :
                  %mi Pb
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_Z:Pb\to Z
            is a universal cone, i.e.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pb
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pb
            is a pullback.
          %h2#fibred_products Fibred Products
          %p
            %strong Under Construction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibred+products"} fibred products
